editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Add +1 at each start and −1 at each end. Between two successive distinct coordinates, the active count stays constant. Measure that span using the previous count, then apply all events at its end as one group. Grouping ensures touching intervals never overlap at a single endpoint.

## Walk through the sample
At coordinate 3 one interval ends as another starts. Applying their combined delta preserves the active count. The covered lengths are 7 on [−1,6) and 2 on [10,12), for 9 total; the peak is 2.

## Complexity
Sorting 2n events takes O(n log n) time; the sweep takes O(n) time and the event list uses O(n) space.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<pair<int, int>> events;
    while (n--) {
        int l, r;
        cin >> l >> r;
        events.emplace_back(l, 1);
        events.emplace_back(r, -1);
    }
    sort(events.begin(), events.end());
    int peak = 0, active = 0, last = events.front().first;
    long long covered = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < int(events.size());) {
        int x = events[i].first;
        if (active)
            covered += (long long)x - last;
        do {
            active += events[i++].second;
        } while (i < int(events.size()) && events[i].first == x);
        peak = max(peak, active);
        last = x;
    }
    cout << peak << ' ' << covered << '\n';
}
